{"from": "passage:lk-01-05", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-02-01", "note": "Both date the setting to Herod king of Judea; Luke 1:5 asserts the existing Matthew 2:1 office claim."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-05-zechariah-priest-abijah", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:1-chronicles-24-10", "note": "1 Chronicles 24:10: 'the eighth to Abijah' - the course of Abijah named in the Davidic list; a biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-15-no-wine-strong-drink", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:numbers-6-3", "note": "Numbers 6:3: the nazirite 'shall separate himself from wine and strong drink' - the biblical vow Luke's language recalls; a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-19-20-gabriel-speaks", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:daniel-8-16", "note": "Daniel 8:16 names Gabriel as the angel of visions; a biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-27-mary-pledged-joseph", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-01-19-betrothal-put-away", "note": "Both Gospels set the conception inside a betrothal; Matthew 1:19 draws the legal consequence (a betrothed woman is 'put away' only by divorce)."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-31-35-mary-conceive-holy-spirit", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-01-18-holy-spirit-conception", "note": "The same conception told differently: Luke 1:31-35 is the annunciation to Mary before conception; Matthew 1:18 is Joseph's discovery after it. Not parallels, not complicates."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-32-33-david-throne", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:2-samuel-7", "note": "2 Samuel 7:12-16, the promise of David's house, throne and kingdom forever - the scriptural background of the prediction; a cross-reference, never evidence for the prediction."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-59-circumcision-eighth-day", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:leviticus-12-3", "note": "Leviticus 12:3: 'In the eighth day the flesh of his foreskin shall be circumcised' - the biblical rule the custom follows; a cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-80-john-in-desert", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-03-01-john-preached-wilderness", "note": "Luke 1:80 covers John's hidden years in the desert; Matthew 3:1 his public wilderness preaching. Luke 3:1-2 will supply the dated appearance."}
{"from": "claim:lk-01-39-city-of-judah", "type": "related_to", "to": "place:ein-kerem", "note": "Luke names no city; Ein Karem is the traditional identification (recorded with a D grade on the place, from Byzantine reception)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-tamid-5-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-08-09-incense-lot", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Tamid 5:2 (redacted c. 200 CE and later, so it may not describe the practice of the 20s CE exactly): the incense was burned by a priest chosen by lot - 'Let only those priests who are new to burning the incense come and participate in the lottery... Whoever won that lottery won the privilege to burn the incense.'"}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shabbat-19-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-59-circumcision-eighth-day", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Shabbat 19:5 (redacted c. 200 CE and later; may not describe the practice of the 20s BCE/20s CE exactly): 'When the eighth day of a baby's life occurs on Shabbat, he must be circumcised on that day' - eighth-day circumcision as standing halakha. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:qumran-mishmarot", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-05-24-divisions", "grade": "B", "note": "Qumran calendrical texts (2nd-1st century BCE) include the mishmarot, 'rosters of weekly shifts or courses for Temple service' - the division system in the period; Wikipedia's article on the priestly divisions cites the mishmarot against Luke 1:5-11, 23."}
{"from": "text:josephus-life-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-05-24-divisions", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus (a priest, c. 100 CE): 'I am not only sprung from a sacerdotal family in general, but from the first of the twenty-four courses' - the twenty-four-course system in living memory of the Temple."}
{"from": "artifact:caesarea-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-05-24-divisions", "grade": "C", "note": "Late 3rd/4th-century CE Hebrew fragments from Caesarea listing priestly courses with their villages; the fragments preserve only partial names, the rest reconstructed - the system after the Second Temple's destruction."}
{"from": "text:psalms-of-solomon-17", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-33-davidic-expectation", "grade": "C", "note": "A 1st-century BCE Jewish psalm asks God to 'raise up unto them their king, the son of David' - the Davidic-king expectation in Judea (reused node); it attests the expectation, never the prophecy's fulfilment."}
{"from": "text:1-enoch-20-gabriel", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-01-19-20-gabriel-speaks", "grade": "C", "note": "1 Enoch 20:7 names Gabriel among the seven archangels - the angel's name was current in pre-Christian Jewish tradition; it attests the name, not that Gabriel appeared to Zechariah."}
{"from": "text:1-enoch-20-gabriel", "type": "attests", "to": "person:gabriel", "grade": "C", "note": "A pre-Christian Jewish text names Gabriel as one of the holy angels, independent of the Gospels; it names the angel, not the annunciation."}
{"from": "text:theodosius-de-situ", "type": "attests", "to": "place:ein-kerem", "grade": "D", "note": "Byzantine reception: Theodosius (530 CE) places 'the town of Elizabeth' five miles from Jerusalem, the Ein Karem tradition; it depends on the Gospel story and is centuries late, hence D."}
